    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Système de cote ÉnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="1" build="19">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.1b19</English>
                <French>v15.1b19</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="11">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.11</English>
                <French>v1.11</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>&lt;hr/&gt;



&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the exterior doors. 



Air leakage was also observed through the two bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your bathroom exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR® rated model.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 110&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;















&lt;hr/&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>&lt;hr/&gt;



The exterior main walls (not including the new addition) of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R22) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 2 inches (R10) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R10 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to your homes main walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 250&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;













&lt;hr/&gt;



</mainWalls>
                <windows>&lt;hr/&gt;



All of the windows within the main home (not including the addition) are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all 10 older windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 420&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;14.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 





















&lt;hr/&gt;

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>&lt;hr/&gt;



When your furnace requires replacing and if you decide to switch to propane as a fuel source, I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 500&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;











&lt;hr/&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>&lt;hr/&gt;



Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 80% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified &lt;b&gt;high-efficiency condensing propane furnace&lt;/b&gt; with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 850&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;26.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;hr/&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>&lt;hr/&gt;



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.84 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $6.58 per bag



&lt;hr/&gt;



&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



&lt;hr/&gt;



&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance&lt;/b&gt;

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Michael.Goodwin@aea.nt.ca



&lt;b&gt;Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;hr/&gt;</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
